17 Abstract 18 19 The world's socio-economic development is continuously increasing the demand for efficient 20 production of food, feed, and energy from the agricultural sector. In this respect, the emerging 21 production of black soldier fly larvae (BSFL) represents a promising system to upscale low22 quality resources to higher-quality resources usable as feed or biodiesel. To optimize BSFL 23 production it is critical to establish methods that are relevant to examine how rearing condi24 tions, including temperature, affect growth, metabolism, and body composition at large scale. 25 In a first set of experiments, we therefore compared how small-scale laboratory respirometry 26 chambers (600 larvae) support gas exchange, growth, and metabolism in black soldier fly lar27 vae (BSFL) with similar measurements obtained from large-scale commercial conditions (>12 28 million larvae) at ~34-35°C. Having observed that small- and large-scale systems generated 29 comparable measurements of growth and metabolism, we used our small-scale respirometry 30 system to examine in detail how rearing temperature ranging from 27.0 to 41.9°C influenced 31 growth, metabolism and body composition in BSFL. Here we found that 7-day growth of 32 BSFL was stable and high at 27, 35, and 39°C, while a rearing temperature of ~ 42°C causes a 33 severe, but sublethal, depression of growth and metabolism. Despite the general similarities in 34 total growth over the 7-day period at 27, 35, and 39°C, we find considerable acceleration of 35 metabolism and development at the two highest rearing temperatures (35 and 39°C) while 36 lowered rearing temperature (27°C) reduces CO2 production per kg larvae produced and the 37 lower temperature is also associated with a higher crude protein to crude lipid ratio in larvae 38 after the 7-day growth period. Based on these findings we discuss how continued monitoring 39 of gas exchange in production systems holds a potential to optimize production in the emer40 ging industry of large-scale commercial insect production.
