45 Introduction 46 47 The continued growth of the human population combined with an expected improvement in 48 global socio-economic conditions will raise the demand for increased agricultural production 49 (Alexandratos and Bruinsma, 2012; Tilman et al., 2011). However, agriculture is a major con50 tributor to climate change through emissions of greenhouse gases (GHG), and agricultural 51 land use has severe consequences for biodiversity (Campbell et al., 2017; Foley et al., 2011). 52 These integrated and intertwined problems challenge the agricultural sector to promote and 53 develop production systems that increase yields, reduce CO2 emissions, improve energy man54 agement, and optimize the recycling of resources. Insect production, and in particular the pro55 duction of black soldier fly (Hermetia illucens) larvae (BSFL), has been promoted as an in56 dustry with the potential to mitigate some of these challenges as it has the potential to recycle 57 or upcycle low-quality organic matter to support the production of food, feed or biodiesel 58 (Cortes Ortiz et al., 2016; Gahukar, 2016; Tabassum et al., 2016). Compared to traditional 59 livestock production, the production of insects emits less GHG, requires less land, and utilizes 60 less water (Gahukar, 2016; Oonincx and de Boer, 2012; Tabassum et al., 2016). Furthermore, 61 this generalist detritivorous insect (Danieli et al., 2019) can bio-convert nutrients from organic 62 waste into larval protein and lipid, which can be used as a supplement in livestock feed (Abd 63 El-Hack et al., 2020; Barragan-Fonseca et al., 2017), or in the case of non-feed grade bio64 masses, as biodiesel and other non-food products depending on national regulations (Kaczor 65 et al., 2023; van Huis, 2022; Zheng et al., 2012). Insect production is also characterized by 66 high feed conversion efficiency and high productivity (Gahukar, 2016). Even so, all produc67 tion systems, including BSFL production, can be optimized through an improved understand68 ing of the abiotic and biotic factors that influence development, growth, and metabolism (Gil69 lott, 2005). 70 Temperature is one of the most important factors influencing the survival, developmental rate, 71 growth, and metabolism of insects (Neven, 2000; Shumo et al., 2019; Jørgensen et al., 2021; 72 Ørsted et al. 2022; Chia et al., 2018). Consequently, rearing temperature also influences feed 73 conversion efficiency (Gligorescu et al., 2018), as well as the proportional emission of GHG, 74 and some studies have indicated that rearing temperature influences the product quality of the 75 insects in terms of lipid-to-protein ratio (Bjørge et al., 2018; van Dooremalen and Ellers, 76 2010). Despite the widespread effects of rearing temperature, regulating temperature in large77 scale production systems can be complicated and costly. Furthermore, convective cool78 ing/heating by ventilation systems must be balanced against several other parameters, includ79 ing a) the metabolic heat production from the larvae and the surrounding microbiomes, b) the 80 prevention of hypercapnia and hypoxia, and c) the need to control humidity to produce larvae 81 and frass (remaining feed and excreta) with desired water content. 82 Considering both the costs of temperature regulation and the potential effects on growth and 83 metabolism it is important to parametrize to what degree developmental temperature affects 84 growth, body composition, metabolism, and CO2 production in BSFL under the conditions 85 that prevail in realistic large-scale production systems. To address this question, we conducted 86 this study in two steps. In the first step, we evaluated if a small-scale, laboratory-based respir87 ometry setup (approx. 0.4 kg of feed with 600 BSFL) resulted in comparable estimates of 88 growth rate, metabolic rate, feed conversion ratio, and body composition as was measured in a 89 large-scale commercial production system (>8,000 kg of feed with >12 million BSFL) run90 ning at the same experimental temperature. In the second step, we used the laboratory-based 91 system to investigate the effect of rearing temperature (ranging from 27.0 to 41.9°C) to meas92 ure survival, larval growth, CO2 emission, O2 consumption, respiratory exchange ratio (RER), 93 crude lipid content, crude protein content, and feed conversion ratio of the BSFL. Based on 94 these results, we discuss optimal rearing temperature in terms of productivity, environmental 95 impact, and larval body composition of protein and lipid. Furthermore, the measurements of


