101 Experimental animals and feed
102 For both large-scale and small-scale experiments, we used the black soldier fly larvae (BSFL, 103 Hermetia illucens), and feed provided by the BSFL producer ENORM Biofactory A/S, Den104 mark. The seed larvae were approximately 5-6 days old, corresponding to the 4th or 5th instar 105 (Gligorescu et al., 2019) at the onset of the experiment and larvae were subsequently reared 106 for 7 days at a density of approximately 1.5 seed larvae per gram of wet pre-mixed commer107 cial feed. Feed was a mixture of by-products from the agricultural and food industry (+70%) 108 including side streams from the potato, milk, citrus, and canola industry, combined with 109 grains to give a wet, porridge-like consistency. The composition of the feed is shown in Table 110 1. 111 112 Table 1. Composition of the commercial feed. 113
114 Experimental protocols
115 The present study had two principal research aims: i) Establish whether the small-scale exper116 imental set-up are indicative of outcomes in large-scale production settings, and ii) to invest117 igate the role of rearing temperature on growth, metabolism, and body composition. 118
119 Experiment 1: Large-scale production setting
120 Large-scale growth and metabolism experiments were performed twice under similar condi121 tions in November 2021 and December 2021 at ENORM Biofactory A/S, Denmark. Respect122 ively, 1.080 and 1.320 crates were placed within a 5 x 10.5 x 2.3 m rearing unit (Figure 1). 123 Each plastic crate (60 cm x 40 cm) contained ~7.5 kg of feed and ~11.200 seed larvae with a 124 weight of ~4-13 mg per larvae. Crates were stacked in racks that allowed ventilation of all 125 crates and the automated ventilation system recorded substrate and air temperature (°C) as 126 well as relative humidity (RH, %), airflow (L/min), and CO2 concentration (%) every 2 127 minutes from the outgoing air. The ventilation system of the rearing unit was programmed to 128 control airflow, temperature and humidity such that substrate rearing temperature averaged 129 ~34°C with fluctuations confined between 32-38°C, and room air was generally maintained at 130 70-90% RH over the 7-day growth period. 131
