170 Experiment 2: Effects of temperature on growth and metabolism
171 In the spring of 2022, a series of experiments were performed to investigate the effect of rear172 ing temperature on the growth and metabolism of BSFL using the small-scale respirometry 173 system. For all experimental temperatures, 580-605 larvae (3.4-6.2 mg per larvae) were 174 placed in ~400 g feed in 6 round 1 L respiration chambers. Each replicate chamber contained 175 a data logger (DS1921G Thermochron iButton, Maxim Integrated, USA) that recorded every 176 30 min to measure the variability and mean temperature. The four treatment temperatures: 177 27.0, 35.3, 39.4, and 41.9°C represent the average of the substrate temperatures recorded 178 across the 7-day growth period. CO2 production and O2 consumption were recorded hourly for 179 7 days with 2 empty chambers for control measurements. At the termination of the experi180 ment, larvae from each replicate were weighed and samples were stored at -20°C for sub181 sequent measurements of fractional water, crude lipid, and crude protein content. 182
183 The small-scale respirometry system
184 O2 consumption rate and CO2 production rate in the small-scale facility were measured using 185 open respirometry in 8 parallel respirometry chambers (diameter 15 cm, height 6 cm, volume 186 1 L) (Figure 2). During experiments, the respirometry chambers were placed in a temperature187 controlled cabinet (Termaks, Norway) programmed to the desired treatment temperature. One 188 or two of the chambers were kept empty to record and control for background concentrations 189 of CO2 and O2 and the remaining chambers contained BSFL and feed. Small mesh nets on the 190 chamber in- and output tubes ensured that larvae could not escape. All respirometry chambers 191 were continuously flushed with air through gastight plastic tubes. Airflow through each cham192 ber was controlled using a Flowbar-8 Mass Flow Meter System (Sable Systems, USA) and 193 airflow was adjusted (range 320-900 mL/min) to ensure a stable and equivalent loss of mass 194 as encountered under commercial conditions. Thus, loss of mass (primarily water) in each 195 respirometry chamber was controlled to ~30 g/day by adjusting airflow (drying) or in some 196 cases by adding MilliQ water. 197


