198 To measure the O2 consumption and CO2 production, a sub-sampler pump (SS-4 Sub-Sampler 199 Pump, Sable Systems, USA) with a fixed rate of 200 mL/min was used to sequentially extract 200 sub-samples of air from the outflow tubes of the 8 respirometry chambers. The sampled air 201 passed through a calcium chloride column for removal of water vapor and syringe filters (Ac202 rodisc syringe filter, 25 mm, 0.2 μm Supor membrane, Pall Corporation, USA) were placed to 203 prevent particulate matter from entering the gas analysers. An interface module (UI-3 Data 204 Acquisition Interface, Sable Systems, USA) was used to program an 8-channel-multiplexer 205 (RM-8 Respirometry Flow Multiplexer, Sable Systems, USA) to sequentially shift between 206 chambers every 7.5 min resulting in one estimate of CO2 production rate and O2 consumption 207 rate from each chamber every hour. Concentrations of O2 were measured using a differential 208 oxygen analyser (FC-2 Differential oxygen Analyser (Oxzilla), Sable Systems, USA), and 209 concentrations of CO2 were measured using a CO2 gas analyser (LI-850 Gas Analyzer, LI210 COR Biosciences, United Kingdom). ExpeData software (Sable Systems, USA) was used to 211 control the 8-channel multiplexer and as an AD converter for data acquisition. Respiration 212 measurements represent the metabolism of several hundred larvae and the interacting sub213 strate microorganisms, which makes it unlikely that the metabolic rate will fluctuate dramat214 ically from hour to hour. Accordingly, outlier data were removed, and a quality assessment of 215 data was performed to prevent faulty measurements from blurring the analysis (see Supple216 mentary material, Figure S1 and S2). 217
218 Small-scale gas quality assessment, estimation, and calculation method
219 The measured flow was corrected to an estimated flow of dry air using a minor correction 220 factor of 0.988 to account for the partial pressure constituted by water vapor in atmospheric 221 air assuming that the ingoing room air temperature was at 21°C and 50% RH. 222 223 The concentrations of consumed O2, Δc(O2), and produced CO2, Δc(CO2), were estimated by 224 the difference in concentration between the respiration chambers, c(O2)chamber and c(CO2)cham225 ber, and an empty control chamber, c(O2)control and c(CO2)control. 226 227 Δc(O2) = c(O2)control – c(O2)chamber (1) 228 Δc(CO2) = c(CO2)chamber – c(CO2)control (2) 229
230 Rates of O2 consumption, V̇ O2, and CO2 production, V̇ CO2, (mL/min) were calculated from the
231 flow rate of dry air through the chamber, V̇ c, and the concentration difference between replic232 ates and empty control chambers, Δc(O2) or Δc(CO2). 233
234 V̇ O2 = V̇ c x Δc(O2) (3)
235 V̇ CO2 = V̇ c x Δc(CO2) (4) 236 237 Total O2 consumption, VO2, and CO2 production, VCO2, (L) for each replicate chamber were 238 calculated for the 7-day growth period by summation of the hourly respiration rate measure239 ment. Quality assessment of the small-scale O2 and CO2 data was performed, and > 90% of 240 the original measurements were considered good quality and used for further data analysis 241 (see Supplementary material). 242 243 The respiratory exchange ratio (RER) was calculated as VCO2/VO2 and the RER estimate is 244 considered to provide information on the combined impact of metabolic respiratory activity 245 and the surplus CO2 expelled during lipogenesis from carbohydrates (Talal et al., 2021). 246


