247 Water, lipid, and protein content
248 Following the 7-day growth period, larvae and frass of individual crates or respirometry 249 chambers were weighed to assess total mass gain of larvae and to assess total loss of mass and 250 water in the feed. Subsequently we processed subsamples of larvae to assess water, crude 251 lipid, and crude protein content of the larvae. 252 253 The water content of either larvae, feed, or frass was determined by measuring the fractional 254 loss of mass following ~24 h of dehydration in an oven set to 70°C. To obtain crude lipid con255 tent, sub-samples of dried larvae were weighed and placed in perforated Eppendorf tubes that 256 were then placed inside a Soxleth apparatus. Here the samples were washed repeatedly with 257 petroleum ether over a 24-hour period to remove the lipid component. The lean samples were 258 then oven dried, and an estimate of the total lipid proportion was calculated from the differ259 ence in initial dry mass and lean dry mass. Another sub-set of dried larvae was taken from 260 each of the replicates to assess crude protein content. The dried larvae were ground with a 261 mortar and samples (~5-10 mg) were analysed for nitrogen content using a combustion ana262 lyser (Na 2000, Italy). Crude protein content was estimated by multiplying the nitrogen con263 tent by a factor of 4.76, which is a standardized conversion factor for protein to nitrogen ratio 264 in BSFL (Janssen et al., 2017). Crude lipid and crude protein content was, for the large-scale 265 experiments, only determined for the second round of experiments. 266
267 Data analysis
268 Data analysis and visualizations were performed in RStudio version 2023.09.1 with R version 269 4.3.2 (RStudio Team, 2023; R Core Team, 2023). Due to the small sample size with the large270 scale setup (n = 2), we were unable to statistically test for differences between the large- and 271 small-scale (n = 7) experiments. 272 One-way ANOVA tests were conducted to test for differences between temperature treat273 ments (27.0, 35.3, 39.4, and 41.9°C). The sample size for each small-scale temperature treat274 ment was n = 6, and assumptions, including normality and homogeneity of variances were 275 checked and met. Tukey’s Honest Significant Difference (HSD) test was used as a post hoc 276 test, and results were considered statistically significant if they met the criterion of p < 0.05. 277
