280 Comparison of growth and metabolism at large- and small-scale
281 In our initial experiments, we compared growth, metabolism, and CO2 production rates meas282 ured at large-scale production conditions (>8,000 kg, >12 million BSFL, n = 2) with values 283 measured under controlled small-scale laboratory conditions (~0.4 kg, ~600 BSFL, n = 7). 284 Formal statistical comparison between these systems was not appropriate given that data from 285 the large-scale experiments were only sampled in two independent replicates. Even so, the 286 data qualitatively supports that small-scale laboratory conditions can be considered represent287 ative of large-scale production conditions (Figure 3 and Table 2). Measurements of gas ex288 change from both small- and large-scale systems found the CO2 production rate to increase 289 slowly in the first 36 hours, after which there was a more potent acceleration in metabolism 290 during the subsequent 48 hours. In both systems, the CO2 production rate peaked after approx291 imately 72-84 hours (Figure 3). Subsequently, the CO2 production rate declined steadily in the 292 small-scale chambers during the following 4 days while this decline was less pronounced and 293 more variable under the large-scale conditions. Integrating the total CO2 production over the 294 7-day growth period yielded a net CO2 production of 65.7 ± 1.0 L CO2 per kg wet mass in the 295 small-scale respiration chambers, while CO2 production was estimated 20-25% higher for the 296 two independent measuring rounds of CO2 production at large scale (82.6 and 78.0 L CO2 per 297 kg wet mass, respectively; Figure 3 and Table 2). Note, however, that measurements of CO2


