330 Effects of temperature on growth and metabolism of BSFL
331 Our initial experiment suggests that larval growth and metabolism in small-scale respirometry 332 chambers are representative of large-scale production systems, and we therefore continued to 333 investigate the role of rearing temperature on growth and metabolism in BSFL at 27.0, 35.3, 334 39.4, and 41.9°C in the small-scale system. As seen from the respiratory data, the dynamic 335 changes in metabolism during the 7-day growth period vary conspicuously with temperature 336 (Figure 4A). All treatments were characterized by sequential increases and decreases in gas 337 exchange that likely reflect how the “average larvae” in the respiration chambers develop 338 through larval instars. However, these transitions occur slower at low temperature (27.0°C) 339 where the final and largest peak in metabolism is reached after 6 days. In contrast, develop340 ment and metabolism peaked earlier at 35.3 and 39.4°C while the developmental and meta341 bolic transitions were more difficult to pinpoint at 41.9°C. Thus, the highest temperature was 342 most stressful limiting metabolism (Figure 4A). When the total metabolic expenditure during 343 the 7-day growth period was assessed from the total O2 consumption we found similar meta344 bolic costs at the three lowest rearing temperatures with O2 consumption of 45.6 ± 2.9, 45.7 ± 345 2.4 or 46.2 ± 2.2 L O2 per kg initial wet mass at 27.0, 35.3 and 39.4°C, respectively. However, 346 metabolism was reduced to less than half at 41.9°C, where the 7-day O2 consumption was 347 only 22.1 ± 2.5 L O2 per kg initial mass (Figure 4B). A similar pattern was found for CO2 pro348 duction, with the exception that total CO2 production was marginally, but significantly, lower


