400 between the two growth systems (Table 2). The results found in the current study do not agree 401 with the findings from previous studies, which found a higher larval weight in big scale 402 (crates of 2,060 cm2) compared to small scale (194 cm2) (Yakti et al., 2022). According to 403 Yakti et al. (2022), the difference is related to a higher substrate temperature in larger crates, 404 which may be avoided in the current experiment because of efficient temperature control in 405 both our small and large-scale systems (measured average was ~34-35°C, and fluctuations in 406 substrate temperature ranged only between 32-38°C). Yakti et al. (2022) also found that crude 407 protein content is higher and crude fat content is lower for larvae reared at small-scale com408 pared to large-scale while we observed very similar body composition in our small and large 409 systems. 410 Unlike the similarities in growth data, we estimated CO2 production per kg initial wet mass to 411 be almost 20% higher in the large-scale system compared to the small-scale system (Figure 3 412 and Table 2). We speculate that a considerable part of this difference can be explained by the 413 different experimental methodologies used to assess gas exchange in large- and small-scale 414 facilities, respectively. Specifically, CO2 production measured at large-scale is experimentally 415 inferior to the controlled laboratory conditions because 1) CO2 concentration in the incoming 416 air is not measured directly (but assumed to be 410 ppm), 2) airflow in the rearing unit is as417 sessed indirectly from a pressure drop across a damper, and lastly, 3) rearing rooms are not 418 completely airtight and the recorded airflow may also be confounded by occasional opening 419 of the entrance door during the 7-day growth period. Considering these methodological differ420 ences, a 20% difference is maybe not surprising between the well-controlled small-scale 421 respirometry chambers and the large-scale commercial facilities. Moreover, when CO2 pro422 duction is recalculated relative to larvae production (g CO2/kg produced dry larvae mass) our 423 systems produce 1,632 ± 46 g CO2/kg dry larval mass for the small-scale systems and 2,164 ± 424 69 or 2,350 ± 282 g CO2/kg dry larvae mass for the measurements at large-scale. Both these 425 estimates are well within the range of previous studies (Ermolaev et al., 2019; Pang et al., 426 2020; Parodi et al., 2020) where CO2 production from small-scale (>15 kg initial feed) pro427 duced between 1,394 to 2,750 g CO2/kg dry larvae mass (see table 2 in Parodi et al., 2020). 428 429 Comparing the large- and small-scale systems qualitatively, it is evident that the temporal de430 velopment of CO2 production was initially similar between large- and small-scale systems, 431 particularly during the first two to three days of accelerated growth and metabolism (Figure 432 3). Conversely, the gradual reduction in CO2 production rate is more modest and variable in 433 the large-scale system which is likely due to a more heterogeneous growth and development 434 in the >1,000 different crates placed in the rearing unit. This differentiation in development is 435 confirmed by facility staff observations, revealing that crates taken from different positions in 436 the rearing room, may differ in developmental progression on days 4 to 6. 437 438 Based on this initial experiment we conclude that the detailed and precise quantitative estim439 ate of CO2 production rate in our large-scale system was likely confounded by experimental 440 constraints. However, the qualitative information was similar between different months in the 441 large-scale system and thus comparatively informative (i.e. the level of CO2 production is in442 formative of the intensity of larval growth). Furthermore, tracking the temporal development 443 of CO2 metabolism remains a powerful method for monitoring metabolism, growth, and de444 velopment in both small and large systems (see discussion below). Finally, the close similar445 ities in survival, growth, and assimilation between the large- and small-scale systems indicate 446 that small-scale respirometry chambers can be appropriate to investigate production-relevant 447 manipulations of BSFL production. This could include effects of temperature, humidity, diet, 448 texture, and microorganism interactions, that could affect growth efficiency in BSFL (Danieli 449 et al., 2019; Harnden and Tomberlin, 2016; Meng et al., 2023; Raimondi et al., 2020). 450


