451 Effects of temperature on survival, growth, and metabolism of BSFL
452 Our study using small-scale laboratory respiration chambers at different temperatures showed 453 that rearing at 27, 35, and 39°C all support efficient growth in BSFL whereas 42°C causes a 454 severe, but sublethal, depression of growth and metabolism. Previous reports of BSFL have 455 found high survival (>80%) in the temperature range of 15-37°C (Chia et al., 2018; Raimondi 456 et al., 2020) while survival at 40°C is ambiguous with both low (Chia et al., 2018) and high 457 (Purkayastha and Sarkar, 2022) estimates reported in the literature. Here we confirm that 458 BSFL survives rearing under production-like conditions at temperatures ranging from 27.0 to 459 41.9°C while we found 100% mortality at 44.4°C (data from this treatment are not included 460 due to the fatal outcome). In our study design, we initiate the growth experiments using ~5-6 461 days old seed larvae, and the high survival at 41.9°C could therefore be linked to the shorter 462 exposure duration at this stressful temperature compared to previous studies (Chia et al., 463 2018). Thus, the consequences of stressful heat exposure are determined both by the intensity 464 (the absolute temperature) and the duration of exposure (Jørgensen et al., 2021). Furthermore, 465 it is possible that later larvae stages are simply more resilient to high temperatures, and it has 466 previously been shown that the susceptibility to heat stress changes considerably between life 467 stages in BSF (Chia et al., 2018; Tomberlin et al., 2009). Despite the high survival at 41.9°C, 468 it is evident that this temperature is stressful, as it causes clear reductions in both growth and 469 metabolism (see below). Production systems with BSFL should thus avoid rearing temperat470 ures above 40°C while the present study suggests that temperatures ranging from 27.0 to 471 39.4°C all support efficient development and growth of 4th to 7th instar BSFL (Gligorescu et 472 al., 2019). Thus, the metabolic rate at all these temperatures seems to end with a decline that is 473 indicative of a transition to a non-feeding 7th instar larvae (Figure 4). Furthermore, larvae size, 474 and total larvae growth after 7 days are fairly similar between 27.0 to 39.4°C (Figure 5B and 475 5C) confirming earlier reports describing temperature optima in BSFL (Chia et al., 2018; Rai476 mondi et al., 2020; Harnden and Tomberlin, 2016). 477 Even if BSFL growth is well supported by a broad temperature range it is important to con478 sider that small differences can be critical to optimize profitability in a large production facil479 ity. Optimization relates both to the efficiency of energy upcycling from cheap “low quality” 480 diets towards assimilation in the “high quality” larvae, but also to reducing the duration of the 481 rearing process. Regarding the efficiency of growth, the energy assimilation in larvae is de482 termined by the relative energy allocation towards assimilation (growth) versus metabolism 483 (costs related directly to growth or maintenance) (Laganaro et al., 2021). This proportional 484 energy allocation varies with larvae age and dietary substrate and is also likely to be influ485 enced by temperature and developmental stage (Laganaro et al., 2021; Bekker et al., 2021). In 486 our experimental design we estimated total growth over a fixed period (one week) and our 487 measurements do therefore not allow for a direct quantitative partitioning of the cost of 488 growth or maintenance metabolism during specific growth periods. Even so, the temporal 489 changes in CO2 production suggest that growth and metabolism are temperature sensitive 490 which may affect the efficiency of the BSFL production in a system where the growth period 491 is fixed. For example, Figure 4A clearly illustrates that the CO2 production rate fluctuates pre492 dictably with three CO2 peaks for each of the 3 lowest temperature treatments (27.0, 35.3, and
493 39.4°C). These peaks are likely representative of growth phases in the 4th, 5th, and 6th larval 494 instars and growth phases clearly proceed faster when larvae are reared at higher temperat495 ures. These temporal patterns suggest that most larvae have entered the 7th non-feeding pre496 pupal larval stage by the end of day 5 in the 35.3 and 39.4°C treatments. In contrast, the aver497 age larvae grown at 27.0°C likely only transitioned to the 7th instar by the end of day 7 shortly 498 before the termination of the experiment. Although larval instars were not assessed directly in 499 this study, these data suggest that continuous monitoring of metabolic intensity is a potential 500 tool to record larval growth patterns and thereby control (and limit) production time. 501


