502 Effect of temperature on body composition
503 BSFL are known to accumulate anhydrous triglyceride (lipid) in the body during the last in504 stars (Arrese and Soulages, 2010; Gligorescu et al., 2019; Talal et al., 2021) and this process 505 may continue even after they have transitioned to the non-feeding stage. Furthermore, it is 506 possible that rearing at different temperatures stimulates some internal biochemical pathways 507 that ultimately affect the larvae body composition in terms of proportional lipid and protein 508 content as has been suggested by findings from mealworms (Bjørge et al., 2018). Considering 509 the experimental design in the present study it is difficult to separate the direct effects of tem510 perature from those of ontogeny (development) since the respiratory trace suggests that larval 511 development has progressed further at 35.3 and 39.4°C than at 27.0°C. Larvae harvested at 512 27°C are characterized by a lower crude lipid content (32.7%) compared to 35.3 and 39.4°C 513 (36.0 and 34.2%, respectively) suggesting that temperature and/or developmental stage might 514 be a contributing factor besides diet (Nguyen et al., 2015) affecting the macronutrient com515 position of BSFL. It is possible that larvae at 27.0°C had less time to process the lipogenesis 516 associated with the latest developmental stages compared to the two warmer treatments (35.3 517 and 39.4°C). This is consistent with the lower RER of 27.0°C larvae (1.10) compared to 35.3 518 and 39.4°C (1.17 and 1.15, respectively) which are indicative of different levels of total lipo519 genesis activity. Thus, lipogenesis from carbohydrates results in a net CO2 production that 520 contributes to the high apparent RER values, and RER values above 1 are common among 521 fast-growing animals (Talal et al., 2021) including BSFL (Parodi et al., 2020). 522
523 CO2 emission
524 Costs of growth (anabolism) and the basic costs of maintenance of larvae are associated with 525 considerable metabolic turnover, and furthermore, there is likely also a metabolic turnover 526 from the microbial community that lives in the feed and frass (Laganaro et al., 2021). As such 527 it is relevant to consider the CO2 budget of BSFL production as this represents an important 528 characteristic of this emerging “green” technology (Parodi et al., 2020). In the current study, 529 we show that the total emission of CO2 per unit of dry larval biomass increased as the temper530 ature increased, showing a significant leap between temperatures of 39.4 and 41.9°C (Figure 531 5D). We speculate that the higher CO2 emission per g larvae produced at higher temperatures 532 is linked to a lower net growth efficiency, indicating that more energy is used for maintenance 533 relative to growth. Additionally, as discussed previously, warmer treatments also facilitated 534 faster development so some larvae at 35.3 and 39.4 °C presumably had >2 days where their 535 growth was halted, and the larvae were primarily utilizing stored energy after transitioning to 536 the non-feeding 7th larval stage. In the case of larvae reared at 41.9°C, CO2 emission per g lar537 vae (Figure 5D) was considerably higher which, in addition to low growth efficiency, could 538 be linked to metabolic costs associated with heat stress such as production of heat shock pro539 teins at the expense of growth (Gonzalez-Tokman et al., 2020). As mentioned previously, the 540 CO2 emissions measured are not produced from larval metabolism alone but include CO2 pro541 duction from microbial metabolism in the feed (Bekker et al., 2021; Parodi et al., 2020). 542 Therefore, it would be relevant to study directly the effects of temperature on microbial meta543 bolism and the contribution of microbial respiration in the overall system. 544
545 Conclusion 546 547 The data collected in this study qualitatively support that small-scale laboratory facilities are 548 representative of large-scale commercial facilities in terms of survival, growth, and metabol549 ism. The present study also demonstrated that the production of BSFL is efficient across a 550 broad range of temperatures from 27.0 to 39.4°C with respect to survival, larvae size, total 551 larval dry mass, and body composition. Repeated CO2 measurements during the 7-day period 552 indicated that larval growth was fastest at 35.3 and 39.4°C suggesting that rearing at high tem


