553 perature could reduce production time. However, the total CO2 emission per unit of dry larvae 554 biomass increased with temperature, rendering 27.0°C as a more favourable rearing temper555 ature when evaluating larval production in terms of the quantity of larvae biomass per L CO2 556 produced over a 7-day period. Further, the larvae from the 27.0°C treatment were character557 ised by higher crude protein content and lower crude lipid content demonstrating that temper558 ature can affect the macronutrient composition of produced larvae biomass. The optimal tem559 perature therefore depends on the specific goals from the producer (fast growth, lowered CO2 560 emission, higher protein content or other key performance indicators) and here we demon561 strate how continuous monitoring of gas exchange in production facilities provides valuable 562 information to monitor the dynamics of growth and body composition in BSFL and how it can 563 be a tool to control and limit production time.


